<speak><prosody pitch="0.91st" rate="1.04">HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is a protocol used for transferring data over the internet, while HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) is an extension of HTTP that adds security features to ensure the integrity and confidentiality of the data being transferred.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.59st" rate="1.02">Here are the key differences between HTTP and HTTPS: 1.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.66st" rate="0.99">**Security**: The most significant difference is that HTTPS encrypts the data being transmitted, making it unreadable to unauthorized parties.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-1.41st" rate="1.03">This ensures that sensitive information, such as passwords and credit card numbers, remains confidential.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-1.38st" rate="1.01">HTTP, on the other hand, sends data in plain text, making it vulnerable to eavesdropping.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.65st" rate="1.00">2.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.98st" rate="1.00">**Encryption**: HTTPS uses a cryptographic protocol (usually TLS or SSL) to encrypt the data being transmitted.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="1.29st" rate="0.98">This means that even if an unauthorized party intercepts the data, they won&apos;t be able to read or modify it without the decryption key.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.87st" rate="1.03">3.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.68st" rate="1.03">**Authentication**: HTTPS requires authentication of the website&apos;s identity and the client (your browser).</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.53st" rate="1.01">This ensures that you are communicating with the intended website and not a malicious imposter.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.98st" rate="0.99">4.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.18st" rate="1.03">**Certificate**: To establish an HTTPS connection, a digital certificate is required.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-1.04st" rate="1.00">The certificate verifies the website&apos;s identity and ensures it has been issued by a trusted authority (such as a Certificate Authority).</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.32st" rate="1.02">5.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.20st" rate="0.99">**Port number**: HTTP typically uses port 80, while HTTPS uses port 443.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.50st" rate="1.00">In summary: * HTTP: plain text data transfer, no encryption or authentication * HTTPS: encrypted data transfer with authentication and certificate verification When you access a website using HTTPS, your browser will verify the website&apos;s identity and ensure that the connection is secure before transferring any data.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.21st" rate="1.02">This provides an added layer of protection against cyber threats like eavesdropping, tampering, and man-in-the-middle attacks.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.34st" rate="1.01">As a result, it&apos;s recommended to use HTTPS for all online transactions involving sensitive information, such as: * Online banking * Shopping * Login credentials * Payment processing In fact, Google favors HTTPS sites in its search rankings, making it an essential consideration for any website that wants to be found and trusted by users.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/></speak>